# Every text file is LF, on disk and in the repository, on every platform.
# `text=auto` alone still lets core.autocrlf convert to CRLF on a Windows
# checkout, which is exactly what makes git print "warning: LF will be
# replaced by CRLF" on every add - it is flagging that mismatch between
# the working copy and what a fresh checkout would produce. Pinning eol=lf
# removes the mismatch instead of just explaining it: checkout matches
# storage matches working copy, on every platform, and the warning has
# nothing left to report. Everything committed so far is already LF-only,
# so this changes no content, only which conversions git treats as
# deliberate.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out so nothing above can ever misdetect one of these and attempt
# a line-ending conversion that would corrupt it.
*.png    binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff2  binary
*.bin    binary

# The golden conformance fixtures are graded byte-for-byte, so their line
# endings must be exactly what the engine emits (LF) on every platform.
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true rewrites them to CRLF on checkout and the
# golden tests fail on Windows.
tst/golden/** text eol=lf

# The JSON fixtures are compared byte-for-byte as well: forecast.json is a
# real api.weather.gov response that JsonPretty has to reproduce exactly, and
# sample-pretty.json is the hand-written expected output. CRLF on checkout
# would break both.
tst/json/** text eol=lf

# The CSV fixtures too: people.csv is reproduced byte for byte by the writer,
# and a line ending is not incidental to a format whose records ARE lines —
# a checkout that rewrote them would change what the fixture tests. bom.csv
# leads with the three bytes of a UTF-8 mark and must keep them.
tst/csv/** text eol=lf

# The XML and HTML fixtures are graded byte-for-byte for the same reason,
# and markup is worse than JSON here: a CRLF inside an element is TEXT, so
# a checkout that rewrote the endings would not merely fail the comparison,
# it would change what the documents say.
tst/xml/** text eol=lf
tst/html/** text eol=lf

# The weather glass compares its whole render against files/expected.out
# line for line, escape sequences and all, and grades its parsing against
# captured API responses. Same reason again.
mills/weather-glass/files/** text eol=lf

# The tally mill's report is graded against files/expected.out line for
# line, and its data and specs are read as text. Its captured charts are
# PNGs and must never be touched — a rewritten line ending inside a
# deflate stream is a corrupt image, not a whitespace difference.
mills/tally/files/*.out  text eol=lf
mills/tally/files/*.csv  text eol=lf
mills/tally/files/*.spec text eol=lf
mills/tally/files/*.png  binary

